    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"![ROAR Logo](images/png/ROAR_LOGO.png)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# ROAR: Robust and Optimal Analog Reuse\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## A Python-Based gm/ID Design Automation Framework for Process-Agnostic Analog IC Design\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Author:** Alec S. Adair  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Submitted to:** IEEE SSCS Code-a-Chip Challenge — ISSCC / VLSI Symposium 2026  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"**PDK:** SkyWater SKY130 (open-source 130 nm)  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Repository:** [github.com — ROAR](https://github.com)  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"**License:** Open-Source\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Abstract\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ROAR (Robust and Optimal Analog Reuse) is an open-source, Python-based analog design automation tool that implements the **gm/ID** (inverse-ID) methodology in a graphical, scriptable, and **PDK-agnostic** framework. ROAR decouples the design process from any specific process technology by operating on pre-characterized **Lookup Tables (LUTs)** — CSV files containing small-signal MOSFET parameters swept across drain current, extracted from SPICE simulation.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This notebook demonstrates the complete ROAR design flow:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"1. **LUT Characterization** — How transistor data is extracted and organized  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"2. **Device Exploration** — Visualizing gm/ID design curves across PVT corners  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"3. **Equation-Based Design** — Sizing a current-mirror OTA with symbolic expressions  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"4. **Iterative Solver** — Resolving cyclic dependencies in a two-stage Miller OTA  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"5. **Multi-Corner Analysis** — Ensuring robust designs across process, voltage, and temperature\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"All examples use the **SkyWater SKY130** open PDK with 9 PVT corners (SS/TT/FF × −25 °C/25 °C/75 °C).\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## 1. Background: The gm/ID Design Methodology\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The **gm/ID** (also called *inverse-ID* or C/ID) methodology, popularized by Silveira, Flandre, Jespers, and Murmann, provides a unified framework for analog transistor sizing that works seamlessly from **weak inversion** through **strong inversion** — unlike the classical square-law model which breaks down in modern short-channel processes.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Key Idea\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Instead of relying on analytical MOSFET equations, the designer works with **measured or simulated** device characteristics, parameterized by the *transconductance efficiency* **gm/ID** (denoted `kgm` in ROAR):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"$$\\\\frac{g_m}{I_D} \\\\quad \\\\text{[V}^{-1}\\\\text{]}$$\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This single variable spans the entire operating region:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **High gm/ID** (> 20 V⁻¹) → Weak inversion → Low power, low speed  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Low gm/ID** (< 10 V⁻¹) → Strong inversion → High speed, high power  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"All relevant design parameters — $f_T$, $g_m \\\\cdot r_o$, capacitance ratios, current density — are uniquely determined by gm/ID for a given device, channel length, and PVT corner. This creates a complete **design space** that ROAR visualizes and optimizes over.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### References\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- P. Jespers and B. Murmann, *Systematic Design of Analog CMOS Circuits Using Pre-Computed Lookup Tables*, Cambridge University Press, 2017.  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"- B. Murmann, \\\"The gm/ID Design Methodology: A Tutorial,\\\" *IEEE Solid-State Circuits Magazine*, 2023.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## 2. Environment Setup\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"import os\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import sys\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import json\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import numpy as np\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import pandas as pd\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import matplotlib\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\",\n",
    "    \"from matplotlib.ticker import EngFormatter\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import warnings\\n\",\n",
    "    \"warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Set up ROAR paths\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ROAR_HOME = os.path.abspath('.')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"os.environ['ROAR_HOME'] = ROAR_HOME\\n\",\n",
    "    \"sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(ROAR_HOME, 'src', 'gui'))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(ROAR_HOME, 'src'))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Import ROAR core modules (headless — no PyQt6 required)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Use Agg backend to avoid display issues in notebook\\n\",\n",
    "    \"matplotlib.use('Agg')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"%matplotlib inline\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"from cid import CIDCorner, CIDDevice\\n\",\n",
    "    \"from equation_solver import ROAREquationSolver\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Plotting style\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.rcParams.update({\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'figure.figsize': (10, 6),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'figure.dpi': 120,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'font.size': 11,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'axes.grid': True,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'grid.alpha': 0.3,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'lines.linewidth': 1.5,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'svg.fonttype': 'none',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"})\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Color palette for PVT corners\\n\",\n",
    "    \"CORNER_COLORS = {\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'ss-25': '#1f77b4', 'ss25': '#2ca02c', 'ss75': '#d62728',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'tt-25': '#9467bd', 'tt25': '#8c564b', 'tt75': '#e377c2',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    'ff-25': '#7f7f7f', 'ff25': '#bcbd22', 'ff75': '#17becf',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"ROAR_HOME: {ROAR_HOME}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"NumPy:     {np.__version__}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Pandas:    {pd.__version__}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"ROAR core modules loaded successfully (headless mode).\\\")\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## 3. LUT Characterization Flow\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ROAR's design methodology starts with **characterization**: extracting MOSFET small-signal parameters from SPICE simulation and storing them in CSV lookup tables.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### 3.1 Characterization Procedure\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A **diode-connected** MOSFET test bench is used (VGS = VDS). A drain current is swept logarithmically (e.g., 1 nA to 1 mA, 20 points/decade), and at each bias point the simulator extracts:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Parameter | Description | Units |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"|-----------|-------------|-------|\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `gm` | Transconductance | S |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `gds` | Output conductance | S |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `cgg, cgs, cgd, cdd, css, cds` | Capacitances | F |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `vth, vdsat, vgs, vds` | Voltages | V |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `kgm` (gm/ID) | Transconductance efficiency | V⁻¹ |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This is repeated for every combination of **device model** × **channel length** × **PVT corner**, producing a structured directory tree:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"```\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LUTs_SKY130/\\n\",\n",
    "    \"├── n_01v8/                    # NMOS regular Vt\\n\",\n",
    "    \"│   ├── LUT_N_150/             # L = 150 nm\\n\",\n",
    "    \"│   │   ├── nfettt27.csv       # Typical-Typical, 27°C\\n\",\n",
    "    \"│   │   ├── nfetss-25.csv      # Slow-Slow, -25°C\\n\",\n",
    "    \"│   │   ├── nfetff75.csv       # Fast-Fast, 75°C\\n\",\n",
    "    \"│   │   └── ... (9 corners)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"│   ├── LUT_N_500/             # L = 500 nm\\n\",\n",
    "    \"│   └── ...\\n\",\n",
    "    \"├── p_01v8/                    # PMOS regular Vt\\n\",\n",
    "    \"├── n_01v8_lvt/                # NMOS low Vt\\n\",\n",
    "    \"└── p_01v8_lvt/                # PMOS low Vt\\n\",\n",
    "    \"```\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### 3.2 PDK Agnosticism\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ROAR's LUT format is **PDK-agnostic**. Any simulator (ngspice, Spectre, HSPICE, Xyce) that can produce the required CSV columns can generate compatible LUTs. ROAR currently ships with pre-characterized LUTs for:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **SkyWater SKY130** (open-source 130 nm)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **IHP SG13G2** (open-source 130 nm BiCMOS)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"New PDKs are registered by simply adding one line to `tech_list.txt`.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 3.3 Loading and Inspecting a LUT\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# Load a single LUT CSV and inspect its structure\\n\",\n",
    "    \"lut_path = os.path.join(ROAR_HOME, 'characterization', 'sky130', 'LUTs_SKY130',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                        'n_01v8', 'LUT_N_500', 'nfettt25.csv')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df_example = pd.read_csv(lut_path, skipinitialspace=True)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df_example.columns = df_example.columns.str.lower()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"LUT: {os.path.basename(lut_path)}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"PDK: {df_example['pdk'].iloc[0]}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"W = {df_example['w'].iloc[0]} µm,  L = {df_example['l'].iloc[0]} µm\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Number of bias points: {len(df_example)}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"\\\\nColumns ({len(df_example.columns)}):\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(list(df_example.columns))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"\\\\nDrain current range: {df_example['id'].min():.2e} A  →  {df_example['id'].max():.2e} A\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"gm/ID range:         {df_example['kgm'].min():.1f}  →  {df_example['kgm'].max():.1f} V⁻¹\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Show first few rows of key columns\\n\",\n",
    "    \"key_cols = ['id', 'gm', 'kgm', 'gds', 'cgg', 'cgs', 'cgd', 'cdd', 'ft', 'vth', 'vdsat', 'vgs']\\n\",\n",
    "    \"available_cols = [c for c in key_cols if c in df_example.columns]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"df_example[available_cols].head(10)\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## 4. Device Exploration — Visualizing the gm/ID Design Space\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ROAR's `CIDCorner` and `CIDDevice` classes automatically compute derived parameters (fₜ, gm·rₒ, current density, capacitance ratios) upon loading a LUT CSV. This creates a rich design space that the engineer can explore.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### 4.1 Loading All Corners for a Device\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"def load_device_corners(model_dir, length_dir):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Load all PVT corner CSVs for a given model and channel length.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    lut_base = os.path.join(ROAR_HOME, 'characterization', 'sky130', 'LUTs_SKY130')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    lut_dir = os.path.join(lut_base, model_dir, length_dir)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    corners = {}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    for csv_file in sorted(os.listdir(lut_dir)):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        if csv_file.endswith('.csv'):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            corner_name = csv_file.replace('.csv', '')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            csv_path = os.path.join(lut_dir, csv_file)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            corner = CIDCorner(corner_name=corner_name, lut_csv=csv_path, vdd=1.8)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            corners[corner_name] = corner\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    return corners\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Load NMOS L=500nm (used in the CM OTA design) — all 9 PVT corners\\n\",\n",
    "    \"nmos_500_corners = load_device_corners('n_01v8', 'LUT_N_500')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Load PMOS L=500nm — all 9 PVT corners\\n\",\n",
    "    \"pmos_500_corners = load_device_corners('p_01v8', 'LUT_P_500')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Loaded {len(nmos_500_corners)} NMOS corners: {list(nmos_500_corners.keys())}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Loaded {len(pmos_500_corners)} PMOS corners: {list(pmos_500_corners.keys())}\\\")\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 4.2 Canonical gm/ID Design Curves\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The four canonical plots of the gm/ID methodology reveal the fundamental design trade-offs:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"1. **gm/ID vs. Current Density** — Maps operating region to bias current  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"2. **fₜ vs. gm/ID** — Speed vs. power efficiency trade-off  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"3. **gm·rₒ vs. gm/ID** — Intrinsic gain vs. power efficiency  \\n\",\n",
    "    \"4. **(gm/ID)·fₜ vs. gm/ID** — Combined gain-bandwidth figure of merit\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"def plot_four_canonical(corners, device_label, figsize=(14, 10)):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Plot the four canonical gm/ID design curves across all PVT corners.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=figsize)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    fig.suptitle(f'SKY130 {device_label} — gm/ID Design Space (9 PVT Corners)',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                 fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    plot_configs = [\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        ('kgm', 'iden',  'gm/ID [V⁻¹]', 'Current Density ID/W [A/µm]',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"         'gm/ID vs. Current Density', False, True),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        ('kgm', 'ft',    'gm/ID [V⁻¹]', 'Transit Frequency fₜ [Hz]',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"         'fₜ vs. gm/ID', False, True),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        ('kgm', 'gmro',  'gm/ID [V⁻¹]', 'Intrinsic Gain gm·rₒ [V/V]',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"         'gm·rₒ vs. gm/ID', False, True),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        ('kgm', 'kgmft', 'gm/ID [V⁻¹]', '(gm/ID)·fₜ [Hz/V]',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"         '(gm/ID)·fₜ vs. gm/ID', False, True),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    colors = list(CORNER_COLORS.values())\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    for ax, (x_param, y_param, xlabel, ylabel, title, xlog, ylog) in zip(axes.flat, plot_configs):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        for i, (corner_name, corner) in enumerate(corners.items()):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            df = corner.df\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            # Filter to valid gm/ID range (0.5 to 30)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            mask = (df['kgm'] > 0.5) & (df['kgm'] < 30)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            x_data = df.loc[mask, x_param].values\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            y_data = df.loc[mask, y_param].values\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            \\n\",\n",
    "    \"            ax.plot(x_data, y_data, color=colors[i % len(colors)],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                    label=corner_name, linewidth=1.2, alpha=0.85)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        \\n\",\n",
    "    \"        ax.set_xlabel(xlabel)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        ax.set_ylabel(ylabel)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        ax.set_title(title, fontsize=11)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        if xlog: ax.set_xscale('log')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        if ylog: ax.set_yscale('log')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    # Single shared legend\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    handles, labels = axes[0, 0].get_legend_handles_labels()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    fig.legend(handles, labels, loc='lower center', ncol=5,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"               fontsize=9, framealpha=0.9, bbox_to_anchor=(0.5, -0.02))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    plt.tight_layout(rect=[0, 0.04, 1, 0.96])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    plt.show()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# NMOS canonical curves\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plot_four_canonical(nmos_500_corners, 'NMOS n_01v8 (L = 500 nm)')\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# PMOS canonical curves\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plot_four_canonical(pmos_500_corners, 'PMOS p_01v8 (L = 500 nm)')\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 4.3 Process Variation Analysis\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A key strength of the gm/ID methodology is the ability to **quantify** how PVT variation affects every design parameter. Let's examine the spread of intrinsic gain and transit frequency across corners.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# Compare fT and gmro at a specific gm/ID operating point across corners\\n\",\n",
    "    \"kgm_targets = [5, 10, 15, 20, 25]  # V^-1\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 5))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"for device_type, corners, ax, param, ylabel, title in [\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ('NMOS', nmos_500_corners, ax1, 'ft', 'fₜ [GHz]', 'NMOS fₜ Across PVT Corners'),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ('NMOS', nmos_500_corners, ax2, 'gmro', 'gm·rₒ [V/V]', 'NMOS gm·rₒ Across PVT Corners'),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"]:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    corner_names = list(corners.keys())\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    data = {kgm: [] for kgm in kgm_targets}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    for corner_name, corner in corners.items():\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        for kgm_t in kgm_targets:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            val = corner.lookup('kgm', param, kgm_t)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            scale = 1e-9 if param == 'ft' else 1.0\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            data[kgm_t].append(val * scale)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    x = np.arange(len(corner_names))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    width = 0.15\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    for j, kgm_t in enumerate(kgm_targets):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        offset = (j - len(kgm_targets)/2) * width + width/2\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        bars = ax.bar(x + offset, data[kgm_t], width, label=f'gm/ID = {kgm_t}', alpha=0.85)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.set_xlabel('PVT Corner')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.set_ylabel(ylabel)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.set_title(title, fontsize=11, fontweight='bold')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.set_xticks(x)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.set_xticklabels(corner_names, rotation=45, ha='right', fontsize=8)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.legend(fontsize=8)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3, axis='y')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.tight_layout()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.show()\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 4.4 Channel Length Comparison\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ROAR's LUTs span multiple channel lengths. Let's visualize how the gm·rₒ vs. gm/ID trade-off changes with length.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# Compare NMOS gm·ro across channel lengths (TT, 25°C corner)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"lengths = ['LUT_N_150', 'LUT_N_200', 'LUT_N_250', 'LUT_N_300', 'LUT_N_500', 'LUT_N_1000']\\n\",\n",
